      <article id="b-article">
        <header class="b-article-header">
          <p class="b-overline">Essay 001 · Systems</p>
          <h1>Building a home for work that is still becoming</h1>
          <p class="b-deck">
            A public blog and a private writing room need different doors, but they can still share
            one editorial discipline.
          </p>
          <div class="b-meta">
            <p>Paolo Cargnin<br /><time datetime="2026-07-31">31 July 2026</time></p>
            <p>8 minute read<br />Last verified 1 August</p>
          </div>
        </header>

        <div class="b-body">
          <aside class="b-margin-note">
            <strong>Reading mode</strong>
            <span>Source Serif’s sturdier texture keeps the narrow measure from feeling precious.</span>
          </aside>
          <p class="b-lede">
            Most publishing tools ask for the finished object too early. They offer a title field, a
            blank editor, and a bright button marked “Publish.” The interface is tidy because all
            the uncertainty has been pushed into the writer’s head.
          </p>
          <p>
            This blog starts from a different premise: an observation is not yet an article, a draft
            is not yet a Post, and a Post should not quietly become something else after readers have
            trusted it. The system should make those distinctions visible without making the act of
            writing feel bureaucratic.
          </p>

          <h2><span>01</span>The boundary is part of the design</h2>
          <aside class="b-margin-note">
            <strong>Public / private</strong>
            <span>The rail can carry definitions or source notes without interrupting the paragraph.</span>
          </aside>
          <p>
            The public repository contains only what a reader may see. Notes, Drafts, reporting, and
            publication candidates live in a separately versioned Workspace. That split is not a
            clever deployment trick. It is a promise that unfinished thinking remains unfinished in
            public, while still receiving the care of history and review in private.
          </p>
          <blockquote>
            <p>Publication is a change of state, not a side effect of saving a file.</p>
          </blockquote>
          <p>
            A deterministic check guards that boundary. It reads the Post contract, validates the
            metadata, and refuses to build when the public record contradicts itself.
          </p>

          <div class="b-code-wrap">
            <p>TERMINAL / VALIDATION</p>
            <pre tabindex="0" aria-label="Example content validation command"><code><span># Verify only material intended for readers</span>
pnpm content:check:public

Validated 12 Posts · 0 unresolved corrections</code></pre>
          </div>

          <h2><span>02</span>A visible trail, kept proportionate</h2>
          <p>
            Not every essay needs the machinery of a newsroom. Every factual claim does need an
            accountable path back to evidence, and every material correction needs a visible date.
            The rigor should rise with the risk while the reading experience stays calm.
          </p>
          <aside class="b-margin-note">
            <strong>Question under test</strong>
            <span>Does visible process increase trust, or does the annotation rail make reading feel audited?</span>
          </aside>
          <p>
            That is the visual test for this prototype too: can navigation, source detail, code, and
            correction history remain available without competing with the sentence currently under
            the reader’s eye?
          </p>
        </div>

